{"ambient_context_auto_assumed": false, "available_prior_named_context": [], "candidate_id": "candidate.stacks.70422ef2cae73c884a44ad15", "candidate_review_status": "needs_actual_mathematical_standalone_reconstruction", "context_events_since_previous_candidate": [{"environment": "lemma", "kind": "enter_local_environment", "line": 5115, "persists_after_end": false, "scope_stack": ["lemma"]}], "explicit_refs": [], "new_refs_in_this_candidate": [], "prior_conclusions": ["For a principal ideal $J \\subset R$, and for any ideal\n$I \\subset J$ we have $I = J (I : J)$."], "prior_context_status": "resolved", "prior_hypotheses": ["Let $R$ be a ring."], "proof_screened": false, "provisional_durable_scope_assertions": [], "schema": "maththm.section-walk-candidate.v1", "section_label": "section-oka-families", "section_opening_preview": "This section is taken from the CRing project. Let $R$ be a ring and\nlet $S \\subset R$ be a multiplicative subset.\nA consequence of\nLemma \\ref{lemma-spec-localization}\nis that an ideal $I \\subset R$ maximal with respect to the property\nof not intersecting $S$ is prime. The reason is that $I = R \\cap \\mathfrak m$\nfor some maximal ideal $\\mathfrak m$ of the ring $S^{-1}R$.\nIt turns out that for many properties of ideals, the maximal ones\nare prime. A general method of seeing this was developed in \\cite{Lam-Reyes}.\nIn this section, we digress to explain this phenomenon.\nLet $R$ be a ring. If $I$ is an ideal of $R$ and $a \\in R$, we\ndefine\n$$\n(I : a) = \\left\\{ x \\in R \\mid xa \\in I\\right\\}.\n$$\nMore generally, if $J \\subset R$ is an ideal, we define\n$$\n(I : J) = \\left\\{ x \\in R \\mid xJ \\subset I\\right\\}.\n$$", "section_start_line": 5089, "section_title": "A meta-observation about prime ideals", "source_filename": "algebra.tex", "source_label": "lemma-colon", "source_revision": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_statement_line": 5115, "source_url": "https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/algebra.tex#L5115", "statement_tex": "\\label{lemma-colon}\nLet $R$ be a ring. For a principal ideal $J \\subset R$, and for any ideal\n$I \\subset J$ we have $I = J (I : J)$.", "statement_truncated": false}
